/**
 * The build loop every unattended agent has to run, in order. It leads each
 * tool result an agent reads before it builds, because agents skipped the same
 * steps when they sat deep inside a long result. [BD-1148]
 */
export const BUILD_LOOP = [
  "Required, in this order, before you present any UI:",
  `1. Design system: keep the product's own design whenever it has one. In a codebase with its own theme file, tokens or component library, build with those, read their values with \`extract-design-system\` if you need them, and never install a catalog system over them. For a product with a live site, call \`extract-from-url\` on that site before you build: it is required, never skipped. With no code at hand, its tokens are its design: use its colours, surfaces, borders, font and radius, and keep the type at app scale, not the site's display sizes. When \`extract-from-url\` returns both themes, ship both, switched with \`prefers-color-scheme\`. When it returns one theme, ship only that scheme. Use the brand's own \`primary\` from \`extract-from-url\` for actions and the selected state. When it reports no primary, the brand has no colour accent: fill the main button with its text colour and label it in its background colour. Never pick a hue of your own, such as red, blue or indigo. When \`extract-from-url\` reports the brand is flat, its tokens give colour, font and radius but no material: take the components from a catalog system with \`find-design-system\` and \`get-design-system-kit\`, then set that system's primary, font and radius to the brand's, so the buttons and cards get the system's material and keep the brand's colour. A product with no design of its own installs a catalog system the same way. Choose one whose colour scheme matches the brand: a light brand gets a light system and a dark brand a dark one, even when a system of the other scheme ranks first.`,
  "2. Principles: call `get-ui-principle` before styling and `get-ux-principle` before behavior.",
  "3. Build with those tokens and principles. A product screen sits inside the product's app shell: navigation with the product's real sections, the current one active, and the workspace or project it belongs to. Show the summary, the records and the selected record's detail together, not behind tabs. Keep the summary to the few numbers a working user acts on: one leads, the rest sit beside it as plain figures or in the page header, never a default row of identical stat cards. The detail shows the full record a working user checks, not a summary of it: for a trace, every span by its real name in a timed waterfall, the selected span's attributes, and model, tokens and cost. Use the record's own names and fields from the product's docs, and give each action one place on the screen. Fill it with realistic sample data, including the metrics, IDs and fields a working user would see: sample data in a product screen is not an invented claim. Only claims about the company itself, such as customers, results and endorsements, must stay real. The main button always looks active: a solid fill and a full-contrast label, never a grey or faded fill. Only a control that is truly disabled looks disabled. Mark a selected row or the active navigation item with a fill, the brand primary at low opacity or the surface's selected tint, never a coloured edge, underline or outline. Keep the page title at app scale, the size of a section heading, not a marketing display. Keep each table row on one line by dropping low-value columns. Truncate only IDs and long free text, never the labels people scan. On a marketing page, put the title and its action beside the product photo, never on top of it behind a wash, and keep the source site's header navigation and footer link groups: the one-action and word limits apply to the hero and sections, not to navigation.",
  "4. Review: where `review-ui-code` is available, run it on every file you wrote, a one-file HTML page included, after your last edit: send each file's path and no content, so it reads the whole file. Then call `get-review-rules`, then `check-comprehension` once per panel with its real copy, the text inside each mock product screen included, and `inspect-spacing` on the rendered page. Fix every critical and serious finding.",
  "Do not present a page that skipped step 2 or step 4. If a step fails, say which one.",
].join("\n");
